You should get: 25Single quotes keep an algebraic expression on the stack without evaluating its symbols. Try 'X^2+1'. EVAL evaluates it using available variable values.

In a native formula field, enter X^2+1 directly; the field already knows it is a formula. RPL programs instead use « and » and contain stack commands. An expression such as X^2 and a program such as « DUP * » are different kinds of object.Square brackets make vectors and matrices. A matrix contains rows, for example [[1 2][3 4]].

Try [[1 2][3 4]] DET to get -2. Matrix addition needs matching dimensions; matrix multiplication needs columns of the first to match rows of the second. Home > Math & graphs > Matrix workbench offers a grid editor and guided operations.Based integers support binary, octal, decimal, hexadecimal and other radices. Bin, Oct, Dec and Hex select common display bases. Base accepts a radix from 2 to 36.

On LumaRPL, Bin, Oct, Dec and Hex also convert an ordinary decimal integer at the top directly. For example, enter 42, then Bin to see 101010; Hex shows 2A. Decimal fractions are not converted. RealToBinary remains available explicitly; BinaryToReal returns a based object to an ordinary integer. WordSize controls fixed-width operations such as shifts and rotations. Changing a displayed base changes the representation rather than the value.DO runs the body before its test, so it runs at least once. Put the body after DO and the stopping condition after UNTIL.

Try 0 « DO 1 + UNTIL DUP 3 < NOT END » EVAL. It stops at 3. Unlike WHILE, a true UNTIL condition ends the loop.FOR repeats a program body while giving a local variable successive values. Put the first and last values before FOR, followed by the variable name. NEXT advances by one.

Try 0 « 1 3 FOR K K + NEXT » EVAL. It adds 1, 2 and 3 to the starting zero, leaving 6. STEP can use a supplied increment instead of NEXT.Use IF inside a program to choose a branch. The code before THEN leaves a condition; a true condition runs the THEN branch and a false one runs ELSE. ELSE is optional.

Try « IF 2 1 > THEN 7 ELSE 9 END » EVAL. The result is 7. Type the structure into a program; the words are not separate calculator buttons.The SD library contains additional programs and equations. At the calculator, type Library and press Enter to browse its program groups. Supply a program's required stack inputs before running it.

A library reference uses the Ⓛ marker and needs the matching library resources on SD. It is not the same as a built-in command. The current reference lists built-in commands and settings; the supplied library definitions remain under /db48x/config and /db48x/library.A list groups objects inside braces, for example {1 2 3}. Elements can be numbers, expressions or other objects.

Try {1 2 3} ListSum to get 6. GET reads an element and PUT replaces one. List positions start at 1. MAP, REDUCE and FILTER apply operations to elements; open their entries for those operations.A local-variable structure takes values from the stack and gives them names for its body. Those names do not replace your HOME variables.

Try 2 « -> WIDTH « WIDTH WIDTH * » » EVAL. The result is 4. Type -> for the arrow. It introduces the local names; the following program is their body. With several names, supply values in the matching order.Open an entry with Enter or F1. Up/Down scrolls its page, Left/Right moves by a page, and F5 or Esc returns to the same list position. F4 Find in the list searches names and accepted spellings; an exact match comes first.

Read an example here, then return to the calculator to type it. Reading or pressing Enter on a help page does not run the example or change your stack. Save important work before trying examples that store variables or change settings.An RPL program is a sequence inside « and ». It can use values already on the stack and leave its answers there.

Try « DUP * » 'SQDEMO' STO, then 12 SQDEMO: the answer is 144. Ctrl+B and Ctrl+E enter the two program delimiters.

Entering a program on its own puts it on the stack. EVAL runs it. A stored name runs its program when evaluated; quoting the name keeps it as a name. Programming > HOME RPL also offers F2 Run. Save the session to keep HOME programs after restart. SD source files have their own Save action.Some setting commands need a value: 3 Fix means three displayed decimal places. Others select a choice without an argument: Rad selects radians. Flags such as BeepOn and BeepOff select opposite behaviors.

Choosing an angle or display mode again does not turn it off. A Beep soft-menu key toggles instead. Save the calculator session with Ctrl+S if you want the current settings after restart. Native documents use their own Save actions.The bottom of a written input sequence is earlier in the stack; the last value you type is on top. Commands use their inputs from there.

For 10 3 -, 3 is on top and is subtracted from 10, leaving 7. SWAP exchanges the top two values and DUP copies the top one. Too few arguments means one or more needed values are missing. Esc dismisses the error so you can supply them.START repeats a body without naming the loop counter. Supply the first and last counter values before START.

Try 0 « 1 3 START 1 + NEXT » EVAL. The body adds 1 three times, leaving 3. Use FOR when the body needs the counter value.Attach a unit with an underscore: 100_cm is a length. To convert it, try 100_cm 1_m Convert. The result is 1 metre. Unit names are case-sensitive and dimensions must be compatible.

Ctrl+U offers From/To lists without having to type unit syntax. Absolute temperatures and temperature differences are different quantities.WHILE tests before running the body, so the body can run zero times. Put the condition between WHILE and REPEAT, and the body between REPEAT and END.

Try 0 « WHILE DUP 3 < REPEAT 1 + END » EVAL. The answer is 3. Keep the values needed by the next condition on the stack.